---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
	title="The Golden Guernsey | Eira Creek"
	description="Discover the history, appearance, temperament, and dairy qualities of the rare Golden Guernsey goat."
>
	<header class="page-hero">
		<div class="container page-hero-content">
			<p class="eyebrow">The Breed</p>
			<h1>The Golden Guernsey</h1>
			<p class="lead">One of the world’s rarest heritage dairy goat breeds.</p>
		</div>
	</header>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container reading-width">
			<h2>A Different Kind of Dairy Goat</h2>

			<p>
				The Golden Guernsey is a breed that people enjoy living with. Their flowing coats
				and warm golden coloring may capture attention first, but their intelligence,
				gentle temperament, and affectionate personalities create lifelong admirers.
			</p>

			<p>
				They are naturally curious, confident, and eager to interact with people. Golden
				Guernseys learn routines quickly, recognize familiar places, and often surprise
				the people who care for them with their memory and determination.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-neutral">
		<div class="container reading-width">
			<p class="eyebrow">History</p>
			<h2>A Story of Survival</h2>

			<p>
				Few livestock breeds have a history as remarkable as the Golden Guernsey.
			</p>

			<p>
				By the early twentieth century, the Golden Guernsey was already a rare breed found
				primarily on the Island of Guernsey in the English Channel. During the German
				occupation of the Channel Islands in World War II, livestock faced an uncertain
				future as food became scarce. Miriam Milbourne protected her small herd from being
				taken and slaughtered, preserving bloodlines that later became central to the
				breed’s recovery.
			</p>

			<p>
				Decades later, North American breeders worked together to import frozen embryos
				and establish Golden Guernsey herds on this continent. Today’s American Golden
				Guernseys continue a story shaped by generations of people who believed this breed
				was worth saving.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container reading-width">
			<p class="eyebrow">Appearance</p>
			<h2>Distinctively Golden</h2>

			<p>
				Golden Guernsey coats range from pale cream to deep golden bronze and may be short,
				long, sleek, or beautifully flowing. Each goat develops its own appearance while
				remaining unmistakably Golden Guernsey.
			</p>

			<p>
				Some grow flowing bloomers or skirts, while others develop striking dorsal fringes
				that resemble mohawks. Golden Guernsey breeders often celebrate these natural
				coats through careful brushing and presentation rather than clipping away one of
				the breed’s most recognizable features.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-neutral">
		<div class="container reading-width">
			<p class="eyebrow">Temperament</p>
			<h2>Gentle by Nature</h2>

			<p>
				The breed is known for being gentle, intelligent, and affectionate. Golden
				Guernseys are naturally curious and confident, with a calm disposition that allows
				them to form trusting relationships with the people who care for them.
			</p>

			<p>
				Their intelligence is easy to see in everyday life. They quickly learn routines,
				recognize familiar places, remember the path to the milking stand, and approach new
				experiences with curiosity.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section">
		<div class="container reading-width">
			<p class="eyebrow">Dairy Qualities</p>
			<h2>Thoughtful Production</h2>

			<p>
				The Golden Guernsey has long been valued as a practical household dairy goat.
				Its moderate size, efficient production, and rich milk make it well suited to
				families, homesteads, and small dairies.
			</p>

			<p>
				At Eira Creek, we measure progress within the breed itself. Our goal is to
				strengthen milk production, udder quality, longevity, structural correctness, and
				overall health while preserving the moderate stature and historic character that
				define the Golden Guernsey.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page-hero {
		padding-block: clamp(6rem, 14vw, 10rem);
		background:
			linear-gradient(rgba(18, 58, 99, 0.76), rgba(18, 58, 99, 0.76)),
			url('/images/hero-goat.jpg') center / cover no-repeat;
		color: white;
	}

	.page-hero-content {
		max-width: 900px;
		text-align: center;
	}

	.page-hero h1 {
		margin-bottom: 1rem;
		color: white;
	}

	.page-hero .eyebrow {
		color: var(--color-accent);
	}

	.page-hero .lead {
		margin: 0 auto;
		color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
	}

	.reading-width h2 {
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}

	.reading-width p {
		color: var(--color-text-soft);
		font-size: 1.08rem;
	}
</style>